    "text": "<p>A current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> enters one corner <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> of an equilateral triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> wires of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> each and leaves by the corner <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The currents <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> in ampere is</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0206_fab3cce49e9a.png\" style=\"width: 140px; height: 181px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For parallel combination, current divides in the inverse ratio of resistance.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.</p>"
